          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Why are varicoceles predominantly left-sided?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Why are varicoceles predominantly left-sided?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The left testicular artery is compressed by the sigmoid colon</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The sigmoid colon does not compress the testicular artery. The cause lies in the venous drainage: the left testicular vein enters the left renal vein at a right angle, impeding venous return.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein at a right angle, creating greater venous resistance</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. This right-angle junction impedes venous return, whereas the right testicular vein drains obliquely into the inferior vena cava with less resistance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The left gubernaculum is shorter, restricting pampiniform plexus expansion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The gubernaculum guides testicular descent during fetal life and does not influence pampiniform plexus dilatation. The cause is the venous drainage angle.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The left cremasteric artery provides less collateral drainage than the right</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The cremasteric artery is not the determinant. The issue is the right-angle junction of the left testicular vein with the left renal vein, which increases resistance to venous return.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key is venous anatomy: the left testicular vein joins the left renal vein at a right angle, creating higher resistance. The right testicular vein drains directly into the inferior vena cava at an oblique angle, so varicoceles are far less common on that side."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The right-angle junction between the left testicular vein and the left renal vein impedes venous return, predisposing to pampiniform plexus dilatation on the left side."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
