          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 14-month-old boy has a right impalpable testis. Ultrasound of the inguinal reg</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 14-month-old boy has a right impalpable testis. Ultrasound of the inguinal region is reported as normal. What is the most appropriate next step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Reassure the parents that the testis is likely absent and no further intervention is needed</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A normal ultrasound does not confirm testicular absence. An intra-abdominal testis is easily missed on ultrasound and requires laparoscopy for definitive evaluation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Repeat ultrasound in 6 months to look for late descent</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Repeating ultrasound delays management without improving detection. Orchidopexy should be completed by 12–18 months, and laparoscopy is needed for an impalpable testis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Proceed with diagnostic laparoscopy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Laparoscopy is the definitive investigation for an impalpable testis. It locates the testis and guides the surgical approach — open orchidopexy or staged Fowler-Stephens.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Prescribe human chorionic gonadotrophin to stimulate descent</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hormonal therapy is not the standard approach for an impalpable testis. Diagnostic laparoscopy is needed to localise the testis and plan definitive surgical fixation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key principle: ultrasound has very limited value for the impalpable testis — a negative scan does not change your approach. Diagnostic laparoscopy is the definitive investigation, and surgery should not be delayed."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. A negative ultrasound does not exclude an intra-abdominal testis. Diagnostic laparoscopy is the definitive investigation for the impalpable testis and also allows therapeutic planning."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
