          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 50-year-old man presents with a painless left scrotal swelling. You cannot get</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 50-year-old man presents with a painless left scrotal swelling. You cannot get your fingers above the swelling at the neck of the scrotum. What does this finding most likely indicate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hydrocele</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A hydrocele is confined to the scrotum, so you can get above it. Inability to get above the swelling indicates extension through the inguinal canal.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inguinoscrotal hernia</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. When you cannot get above the swelling, it extends through the inguinal canal into the abdomen, which is characteristic of an inguinoscrotal hernia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Testicular tumour</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A testicular tumour is confined to the scrotum. You can get above it, even though it will not transilluminate and the testis may feel replaced by the mass.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Epididymal cyst</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>An epididymal cyst is a scrotal swelling; you can get above it. It typically transilluminates and is palpated separately from the body of the testis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The first bedside step is: can you get above the swelling? If you cannot, the swelling extends through the inguinal canal, pointing to an inguinoscrotal hernia. Hydroceles, tumours, and cysts are all confined to the scrotum."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The first bedside test — can you get above the swelling? — distinguishes scrotal swellings from inguinoscrotal hernias that extend through the inguinal canal."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
