          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which serum tumour marker, if elevated, immediately excludes a diagnosis of pure</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which serum tumour marker, if elevated, immediately excludes a diagnosis of pure seminoma?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Beta human chorionic gonadotrophin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Beta-HCG can be modestly raised in some seminomas, so its elevation does not exclude pure seminoma. Alpha-fetoprotein is the discriminating marker.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lactate dehydrogenase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>LDH reflects tumour bulk and can be raised in any testicular tumour type. It does not discriminate between seminoma and non-seminomatous tumours.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Alpha-fetoprotein</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. AFP is never raised in pure seminoma. Any AFP elevation reclassifies the tumour as non-seminomatous, regardless of the histological appearance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Carcinoembryonic antigen</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CEA is a gastrointestinal tumour marker and has no role in testicular tumour workup. The three relevant markers are AFP, beta-HCG, and LDH.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Alpha-fetoprotein is the key discriminator. It is raised in non-seminomatous germ-cell tumours, particularly yolk-sac and embryonal subtypes, and is never raised in pure seminoma — any elevation rules out that diagnosis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Alpha-fetoprotein is never raised in pure seminoma. An elevated AFP immediately reclassifies the tumour as non-seminomatous and changes the treatment pathway."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
